<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's an Orange Cat. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors in Our World</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's an Orange Cat.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Name primary and secondary colors. Interactive: Express likes or dislikes using simple words and pointing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe objects using colors and simple sentences (e.g., "That's a blue bird."). Can answer questions about colors and objects, using one or two-word responses (e.g., "What color is it?" "Blue."). Can express likes and dislikes, using simple words and gestures (e.g., "I like red.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Color Hunt" in the classroom. Students look for objects of specific colors (red, blue, green, orange). The teacher points to a red object and says, "Look! A red flower!" Students repeat and point to other red objects. This activates vocabulary from previous lessons and connects to the theme "It's an Orange Cat." and "Colors in Our World."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards of objects with colors (e.g., a blue bird, a green leaf, an orange cat). The teacher clearly states, "It's a blue bird." "It's a green leaf." "It's an orange cat." The teacher uses TPR by pointing to the object and color. The teacher models the pronunciation of /l/ in "blue" and "leaf," and the initial sound /b/ in "bird" and /k/ in "cat." Students listen and imitate chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple questions and using gestures.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a blue bird flashcard: "Is it a red bird?" (Students shake heads or say "No.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows an orange cat flashcard: "What color is it?" (Students say "Orange!") "Is it a dog?" (Students shake heads or say "No.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors in Our World."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful picture storybook or a digital presentation showing various objects (bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky) with their corresponding colors and simple sentences like "That is a blue sky." or "I see a red flower." Ensure the "orange cat" is prominent.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Recognizing colors and objects: Identify familiar objects by color (e.g., "blue bird" or "red flower."). Describing with colors: Use simple color phrases in sentences (e.g., "That is a red bird.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> Teacher points to a page with many colors and asks, "What do you see?" (Students respond with single words like "cat," "flower," "blue," "red.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Show me:</b> The teacher says, "Show me the green tree!" or "Show me the orange cat!" Students point to the correct image in the storybook/presentation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identify color:</b> Teacher points to an object in the book and asks, "What color is this leaf?" (Students say "Green!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using "Color + noun" phrases and "What color is it?" questions, and expressing likes/dislikes. Grammar: "Color + noun" (e.g., "blue bird"), "WH-questions" (e.g., "What color is it?"), "Present simple" (e.g., "It is an orange cat."). Vocabulary: bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky; blue, purple, orange, green, red; see, look, be (is), like. Pronunciation: /l/ in "blue" and "leaf"; phonemic awareness of initial sounds /b/, /k/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - "Listen and Repeat":</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of various colored objects. Teacher says the full sentence, "It is a red flower." Students repeat chorally. Then, the teacher says, "What color is it?" and students respond, "Red!" Teacher repeats with "It is a blue bird," "It is an orange cat," etc. Emphasize /l/, /b/, /k/ sounds.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - "I Like It" Game:</b> The teacher shows a flashcard (e.g., a green tree). Teacher asks, "Do you like green?" Students who like green stand up and say, "I like green!" or "Yes, green!" Students who don't like it shake their heads and say, "No." This practices expressing likes/dislikes with simple words and gestures.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Color Match":</b> Students work in pairs with a set of small colored object cards (e.g., 2 red flowers, 2 blue birds, 2 orange cats). Student A picks a card, shows it to Student B, and asks, "What color is it?" Student B identifies the color and object, "It's a blue bird." Student A then asks, "Do you like it?" Student B responds, "Yes, I like blue!" or "No, I don't like green." They switch roles. The teacher circulates, offering support and encouraging clear pronunciation.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors in Our World."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Our Colorful World" Show and Tell (Pair Work)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students bring a small, colored object from home or select one from a collection of classroom objects (e.g., a red crayon, a blue toy car, a green leaf, a picture of an orange cat). In pairs, students take turns presenting their object to their partner. Student A shows their object and says, "Look! It's a [color] [object]." (e.g., "It's a red flower."). Student B then asks, "What color is it?" and "Do you like it?" Student A answers (e.g., "It's red!" "Yes, I like red!"). They then switch roles. This task directly connects to the theme "It's an Orange Cat." and the scenario "Colors in Our World," allowing students to use the target language in a meaningful, interactive way. The teacher encourages natural interaction and communication over perfect accuracy, focusing on fluency and task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes students during the "Our Colorful World" Show and Tell activity, using a simple checklist to note their participation and use of target language. Focus is on individual students' ability to name colors and objects, ask "What color is it?", and express likes/dislikes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear enough to be understood (especially /l/, /b/, /k/ sounds).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Accurately uses at least 3-4 color words and 2-3 object nouns.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> Can form simple sentences like "It's a [color] [object]" and "I like [color]."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively participates in pair interaction, asking and answering questions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students receive a paper with three simple emoji faces (happy, neutral, sad). They draw a circle around the face that shows how they feel about saying colors and objects in English. Underneath, they complete the sentence: "Today I can say: ____ (e.g., 'red cat')."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new colors can you say in English?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is your favorite colored object to talk about?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking-focused lesson? The "Our Colorful World" Show and Tell activity successfully encouraged students to use full sentences and interact with their partners. The explicit pronunciation practice of /l/, /b/, /k/ sounds also seemed beneficial.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next lesson? Some students still hesitate with "Do you like it?" questions. For the next lesson (Writing), I will need to provide sentence frames and clear models for writing color and object descriptions. A visual word bank will be essential for sentence construction.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson provided ample oral practice describing objects with colors. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on this by having students write simple sentences describing colors and objects they discussed today (e.g., "It is a red flower," "I like the blue bird"), utilizing the same vocabulary and grammar structure.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>